Today I'm going to talk about Germany's dream airport in Berlin.

196
00:23:13,350 --> 00:23:19,190
The airport looks exactly like every other major modern airport in Europe, except

197
00:23:19,190 --> 00:23:20,770
for one big problem.

198
00:23:21,270 --> 00:23:26,850
More than seven years after it was originally supposed to open, it still stands

199
00:23:26,850 --> 00:23:34,050
empty. Germany is known for its efficiency and refined engineering, but when it

200
00:23:34,050 --> 00:23:40,710
comes to its new ghost airport, this reputation could not be further from the truth.

201
00:23:41,410 --> 00:23:47,870
Plagued by long delays, perpetual mismanagement, and ever -soaring costs, the

202
00:23:47,870 --> 00:23:54,470
airport has become something of a joke among Germans and a source of frustration for

203
00:23:54,470 --> 00:23:58,810
local politicians, business leaders, and residents alike.

204
00:23:59,430 --> 00:24:03,070
Planning for the new airport began in 1989.

205
00:24:03,630 --> 00:24:09,970
At the time, it became clear that the newly reunified Berlin would need a modern

206
00:24:09,970 --> 00:24:14,950
airport with far greater capacity than its existing airports.

207
00:24:15,350 --> 00:24:19,750
The city broke ground on the new airport in 2006.

208
00:24:19,750 --> 00:24:26,370
The first major sign of problems came in summer 2010, when the Construction

209
00:24:26,370 --> 00:24:32,930
Corporation pushed the opening from October 2011 to June 2012.

210
00:24:33,490 --> 00:24:40,090
In 2012, the city planned an opening ceremony, but less than a month beforehand,

211
00:24:40,090 --> 00:24:46,450
inspectors found significant problems with the fire safety system and pushed the

212
00:24:46,450 --> 00:24:49,590
opening back again to 2013.

213
00:24:50,750 --> 00:24:56,650
wasn't just the smoke system, many other major problems subsequently emerged.

214
00:24:57,330 --> 00:25:05,010
More than 90 meters of cable were incorrectly installed, 4,000 doors were wrongly

215
00:25:05,010 --> 00:25:11,570
numbered, escalators were too short, and there was a shortage of check -in desks.

216
00:25:11,750 --> 00:25:17,610
So why, with so many problems discovered, didn't the Airport Corporation decide to

217
00:25:17,610 --> 00:25:20,150
give up on the project and start over?

218
00:25:20,490 --> 00:25:21,830
The reason is simple.

219
00:25:22,130 --> 00:25:28,190
People are often hesitant to terminate a project when they've already invested time

220
00:25:28,190 --> 00:25:33,770
or resources into it, even if it might make logical sense to do so.

221
00:25:33,770 --> 00:25:38,910
The longer the delays continued, the more problems inspectors found.

222
00:25:40,090 --> 00:25:46,090
of the Planning Corporation has changed hands nearly as many times as the opening

223
00:25:46,090 --> 00:25:47,830
date has been pushed back.

224
00:25:48,390 --> 00:25:53,190
Initially, rather than appointing a general contractor to run the project,

225
00:25:53,890 --> 00:25:59,990
the Corporation decided to manage it themselves, despite lack of experience with an

226
00:25:59,990 --> 00:26:01,890
undertaking of that scale.

227
00:26:01,890 --> 00:26:08,750
To compound the delays, unused airport is resulting in massive costs.

228
00:26:09,310 --> 00:26:15,570
Every month it remains unopened, costs between 9 and 10 million euros.

229
00:26:16,190 --> 00:26:23,670
Assuming all goes well, the airport should open in October 2020, but the still empty

230
00:26:23,670 --> 00:26:29,870
airport stands as the biggest embarrassment to Germany's reputation for efficiency

231
00:26:29,870 --> 00:26:35,030
and a continuing drain on city and state resources.

